  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# 3.09 — Minimal Surfaces\n\nStage 3 has been about $K$. This last notebook is about the other curvature.\n\nA **minimal surface** is one with\n\n$$H = \\tfrac12(\\kappa_1 + \\kappa_2) = 0 \\quad\\text{everywhere}.$$\n\nThe name is a promise about area. Deform a surface by moving each point a small distance $\\varepsilon\\,\\phi\\,n$ along the normal; the **first variation of area** works out to\n\n$$\\frac{d}{d\\varepsilon}\\text{Area}\\bigg|_{\\varepsilon=0} = -2\\iint_S \\phi\\,H\\,dA .$$\n\nSo area is stationary under *every* deformation exactly when $H \\equiv 0$. Minimal surfaces are the critical points of the area functional — the Euler–Lagrange equation of a variational problem, in the same sense that geodesics are critical points of length.\n\n**Hence soap films.** A film spanning a wire loop minimises surface energy, which is proportional to area, so it settles into a minimal surface. Every example below can be made with wire and washing-up liquid.\n\n**A warning about the name.** $H = 0$ makes area *stationary*, not necessarily *minimal* — it is a critical point, which may be a saddle. We compute an explicit case below where a minimal surface is beaten by a competitor, which is why soap films sometimes snap into a different configuration.\n\n**Immediate consequences.** $H = 0$ means $\\kappa_2 = -\\kappa_1$, so\n\n$$K = \\kappa_1\\kappa_2 = -\\kappa_1^2 \\le 0 .$$\n\nMinimal surfaces are never positively curved — every point is a saddle or flat. Combined with Gauss–Bonnet this rules a great deal out: there is **no compact minimal surface without boundary** in $\\mathbb R^3$, since $\\iint K\\,dA \\le 0$ would have to equal $2\\pi\\chi > 0$ for a sphere. Soap films need wire.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nload(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\n\ncross3(a,b) := [a[2]*b[3]-a[3]*b[2], a[3]*b[1]-a[1]*b[3], a[1]*b[2]-a[2]*b[1]]$\npd(s,var)   := map(lambda([e], diff(e, var)), s)$\nts(e)       := trigsimp(e)$\nFFF(s,u_,v_) := block([su,sv], su : pd(s,u_), sv : pd(s,v_),\n  matrix([ts(su.su), ts(su.sv)], [ts(su.sv), ts(sv.sv)]))$\nunitnormal(s,u_,v_) := block([n], n : cross3(pd(s,u_), pd(s,v_)),\n  map(ts, n/sqrt(ts(n . n))))$\nSFF(s,u_,v_) := block([nn,suu,suv,svv],\n  nn : unitnormal(s,u_,v_),\n  suu : pd(pd(s,u_),u_), suv : pd(pd(s,u_),v_), svv : pd(pd(s,v_),v_),\n  matrix([ts(suu.nn), ts(suv.nn)], [ts(suv.nn), ts(svv.nn)]))$\nKof(s,u_,v_) := ts( determinant(SFF(s,u_,v_))/determinant(FFF(s,u_,v_)) )$\nHof(s,u_,v_) := block([I1,I2], I1 : FFF(s,u_,v_), I2 : SFF(s,u_,v_),\n  ts( (I1[1][1]*I2[2][2] - 2*I1[1][2]*I2[1][2] + I1[2][2]*I2[1][1])\n      /(2*determinant(I1)) ) )$\n\n/* --- The minimal surface equation for a graph z = f(x,y) --- */\ndepends(f, [x,y])$\ngraph : [x, y, f]$\nHgraph : ratsimp( Hof(graph, x, y) )$\nprint(\"for a graph z = f(x,y):\")$\nprint(\"   H = \", Hgraph)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"so H = 0 is the second-order PDE\")$\nprint(\"   (1 + f_y²) f_xx − 2 f_x f_y f_xy + (1 + f_x²) f_yy = 0\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"numerator of 2H = \", ratsimp( num(Hgraph) ))$\nprint(\"   — matching the equation above ✓\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Note it is NOT the Laplace equation: the nonlinear terms are what\")$\nprint(\"make minimal surfaces hard, and Plateau's problem a real theorem.\")$\nprint(\"For nearly-flat surfaces (f_x, f_y small) it linearises to f_xx + f_yy = 0.\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 1,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "for a graph z = f(x,y):\n   H =\n       ((('diff(f,x,1))^2+1)*'diff(f,y,2)+'diff(f,x,2)*('diff(f,y,1))^2\n                                         -2*'diff(f,x,1)*'diff(f,x,1,y,1)\n                                           *'diff(f,y,1)+'diff(f,x,2))\n        /(sqrt(('diff(f,y,1))^2+('diff(f,x,1))^2+1)\n         *(2*('diff(f,y,1))^2+2*('diff(f,x,1))^2+2))\nso H = 0 is the second-order PDE\n   (1 + f_y²) f_xx − 2 f_x f_y f_xy + (1 + f_x²) f_yy = 0\nnumerator of 2H =\n                  (('diff(f,x,1))^2+1)*'diff(f,y,2)\n                   +'diff(f,x,2)*('diff(f,y,1))^2\n                   -2*'diff(f,x,1)*'diff(f,x,1,y,1)*'diff(f,y,1)+'diff(f,x,2)\n   — matching the equation above ✓\nNote it is NOT the Laplace equation: the nonlinear terms are what\nmake minimal surfaces hard, and Plateau's problem a real theorem.\nFor nearly-flat surfaces (f_x, f_y small) it linearises to f_xx + f_yy = 0."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* --- Two classical solutions of the minimal surface equation --- */\nmineq(ff) := ratsimp( (1 + diff(ff,y)^2)*diff(ff,x,2)\n                      - 2*diff(ff,x)*diff(ff,y)*diff(ff,x,1,y,1)\n                      + (1 + diff(ff,x)^2)*diff(ff,y,2) )$\n\n/* Scherk's first surface: z = ln(cos y / cos x) */\nscherk : log(cos(y)/cos(x))$\nprint(\"Scherk's surface  z = ln(cos y / cos x):\")$\nprint(\"   f_x = \", ratsimp(diff(scherk,x)), \"     f_y = \", ratsimp(diff(scherk,y)))$\nprint(\"   minimal surface equation residual = \", trigsimp(mineq(scherk)), \" ✓\")$\nprint(\"   (defined only where cos x and cos y share a sign — on a chequerboard\")$\nprint(\"    of squares, which is why it is called a doubly periodic surface)\")$\n\n/* Enneper's surface, as a parametrised surface rather than a graph */\nenneper : [u - u^3/3 + u*v^2, v - v^3/3 + v*u^2, u^2 - v^2]$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Enneper's surface:\")$\nprint(\"   I  = \", FFF(enneper,u,v))$\nprint(\"   II = \", SFF(enneper,u,v))$\nprint(\"   H  = \", Hof(enneper,u,v), \" ✓\")$\nprint(\"   K  = \", Kof(enneper,u,v), \"   — negative everywhere, as it must be\")$\n\n/* Minimal ⟹ K ≤ 0, checked on the three surfaces we have */\ncatenoid : [cosh(u)*cos(v), cosh(u)*sin(v), u]$\nhelicoid : [sinh(u)*cos(v), sinh(u)*sin(v), v]$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   surface     H              K\")$\nfor su in [[\"catenoid\", catenoid], [\"helicoid\", helicoid], [\"Enneper \", enneper]] do\n  print(\"   \", su[1], \"   \", Hof(su[2],u,v), \"        \", Kof(su[2],u,v))$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"H = 0 forces κ₂ = −κ₁, hence K = −κ₁² ≤ 0: no minimal surface is\")$\nprint(\"ever positively curved. Every point is a saddle, or flat.\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 2,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "Scherk's surface  z = ln(cos y / cos x):\n   f_x =  sin(x)/cos(x)      f_y =  -(sin(y)/cos(y))\n   minimal surface equation residual =  0  ✓\n   (defined only where cos x and cos y share a sign — on a chequerboard\n    of squares, which is why it is called a doubly periodic surface)\nEnneper's surface:\n   I  =\n        matrix([v^4+(2*u^2+2)*v^2+u^4+2*u^2+1,0],\n               [0,v^4+(2*u^2+2)*v^2+u^4+2*u^2+1])\n   II =\n        matrix([(2*v^4+(4*u^2+4)*v^2+2*u^4+4*u^2+2)\n                 /sqrt(v^8+(4*u^2+4)*v^6+(6*u^4+12*u^2+6)*v^4\n                          +(4*u^6+12*u^4+12*u^2+4)*v^2+u^8+4*u^6+6*u^4+4*u^2\n                          +1),0],\n               [0,\n                -((2*v^4+(4*u^2+4)*v^2+2*u^4+4*u^2+2)\n                 /sqrt(v^8+(4*u^2+4)*v^6+(6*u^4+12*u^2+6)*v^4\n                          +(4*u^6+12*u^4+12*u^2+4)*v^2+u^8+4*u^6+6*u^4+4*u^2\n                          +1))])\n   H  =  0  ✓\n   K  =\n        -(4/(v^8+(4*u^2+4)*v^6+(6*u^4+12*u^2+6)*v^4\n                +(4*u^6+12*u^4+12*u^2+4)*v^2+u^8+4*u^6+6*u^4+4*u^2+1))\n           — negative everywhere, as it must be\n   surface     H              K\n    catenoid     0          -(1/cosh(u)^4)\n    helicoid     0          -(1/cosh(u)^4)\n    Enneper      0\n   -(4/(v^8+(4*u^2+4)*v^6+(6*u^4+12*u^2+6)*v^4+(4*u^6+12*u^4+12*u^2+4)*v^2+u^8\n           +4*u^6+6*u^4+4*u^2+1))\nH = 0 forces κ₂ = −κ₁, hence K = −κ₁² ≤ 0: no minimal surface is\never positively curved. Every point is a saddle, or flat."
          ]
        }
      ]
