    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Summary\n\n$$H = 0 \\iff \\text{area is stationary} \\iff (1+f_y^2)f_{xx} - 2f_xf_yf_{xy} + (1+f_x^2)f_{yy} = 0$$\n\n| Surface | $H$ | $K$ |\n|---|---|---|\n| plane | $0$ | $0$ |\n| catenoid | $0$ | $-1/\\cosh^4 u$ |\n| helicoid | $0$ | $-1/\\cosh^4 u$ |\n| Enneper | $0$ | $-4/(1+u^2+v^2)^4$ |\n| Scherk $z = \\ln(\\cos y/\\cos x)$ | $0$ | — |\n\nEstablished by computation:\n\n- The minimal surface equation was derived from $H = 0$ for a graph, and is **nonlinear** — it linearises to Laplace's equation only for nearly-flat surfaces. That nonlinearity is why Plateau's problem was open until Douglas and Radó solved it in 1930–31.\n- Scherk's and Enneper's surfaces satisfy it identically.\n- $H = 0 \\Rightarrow K = -\\kappa_1^2 \\le 0$: minimal surfaces are never positively curved, and by Gauss–Bonnet there is **no closed minimal surface** in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n- The catenoid is the **only** minimal surface of revolution: $H = 0$ reduces to $ff'' = 1 + f'^2$, whose solution is the catenary.\n- The **associate family** $X_\\theta = \\cos\\theta\\,(\\text{catenoid}) + \\sin\\theta\\,(\\text{helicoid})$ has, for every $\\theta$, the same $\\mathrm{I} = \\mathrm{diag}(\\cosh^2u, \\cosh^2u)$, the same $K$, and $H = 0$ — a continuous isometric deformation through minimal surfaces.\n- **Minimal $\\ne$ minimising.** The soap film between rings of radius 1 at $\\pm h$: the two-disc competitor becomes cheaper at $h = 0.52770$, while the catenoid survives until $h = 0.66274$. In between, a genuine minimal surface is not a minimum.\n\n## Stage 3 complete\n\nNine notebooks, and one thread running through all of them.\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| 3.01–3.02 | curves: $\\kappa$ and $\\tau$, complete invariants up to $SE(3)$ |\n| 3.03 | the metric $\\mathrm{I}$ — everything measurable from inside |\n| 3.04 | the shape operator $\\mathrm{I\\!I}$ — how the surface bends in space |\n| 3.05 | $K$ and $H$, and how differently they behave |\n| **3.06** | **$K$ is intrinsic** — the ambient space was scaffolding |\n| 3.07 | geodesics, written in Christoffel symbols, hence intrinsic too |\n| **3.08** | **$\\int K$ is topological** — geometry drops out entirely |\n| 3.09 | $H$ instead: variational problems, soap films |\n\nThe stage began by abandoning Klein's homogeneous spaces because most surfaces have no symmetry. It ends having built a genuinely local theory — and then discovered that integrating the local data recovers global, topological facts.\n\n**What Stage 4 does with this.** Every construction here needed a surface *in $\\mathbb R^3$*. The Theorema Egregium showed that for $K$ the ambient space is unnecessary; Riemann's move is to drop it for everything. A **Riemannian manifold** is a space with a metric $g_{ij}$ and nothing else — no outside. Then:\n\n$$E,F,G \\to g_{ij}, \\qquad \\Gamma^k_{ij} \\text{ unchanged}, \\qquad K \\to \\text{Riemann tensor}, \\qquad \\sqrt{EG-F^2} \\to \\sqrt{\\det g}.$$\n\nCurves and surfaces become the cases $n = 1, 2$. And because no ambient space is required, the theory works for the four-dimensional curved spacetime of 6.02, which is not sitting inside anything.\n\n## Next\n\n**Stage 4 opens with 4.00 — Manifolds Recap**, a bridging notebook: charts, tangent vectors as derivations, vector fields and forms, cross-linked to [topology-journey](https://github.com/cmsd2/topology-journey) Stage 3 rather than re-derived.\n\n## References\n\n- do Carmo, M.P. (1976) *Differential Geometry of Curves and Surfaces*. Englewood Cliffs: Prentice-Hall. §5-11 on minimal surfaces, including the first variation of area.\n- Pressley, A. (2010) *Elementary Differential Geometry*. 2nd edn. Springer Undergraduate Mathematics Series. London: Springer. Chapter 12, which works the catenoid–helicoid family and the soap-film comparison in detail.\n- Osserman, R. (1986) *A Survey of Minimal Surfaces*. New York: Dover. The standard reference, including the Weierstrass–Enneper representation that generates all minimal surfaces from holomorphic data.\n- Douglas, J. (1931) 'Solution of the problem of Plateau', *Transactions of the American Mathematical Society*, 33(1), pp. 263–321. The solution that won the first Fields Medal in 1936, shared with Radó's independent proof.\n- ★ Lee, J.M. (2018) *Introduction to Riemannian Manifolds*. Cham: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9. Chapter 8 for mean curvature of submanifolds in general.\n\n**The Weierstrass–Enneper representation**, not pursued here, expresses every minimal surface in terms of a holomorphic function and a meromorphic one — making minimal surface theory a branch of complex analysis. The catenoid, helicoid and Enneper surfaces all arise from strikingly simple data. That connection is the reason 6.04's complex geometry is a natural continuation.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = in the local indexed library (`maths` collection); the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell in this notebook was executed and its output is the real result; the prose is written from knowledge and checked against these sources where they cover the material.*",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}